--TEST--
A hole already holding a `Core\Html\Markup` is spliced raw, which is `Markup + Markup` written in interpolation syntax — `rule:core-classes/html-template`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A hole holding a carrier is spliced as it stands: the fragment passed
// whichever rule made it a `Markup`, so escaping it here would corrupt the
// markup it was built for.
var $name = "ada & <grace>";
Core\Html\Markup $inner = html`<em>{$name}</em>`;
echo html`<p>{$inner}</p>`, "\n";

// The data inside the spliced fragment was escaped once, where it was
// written, and the splice does not escape it a second time — `&amp;` stays
// `&amp;` rather than becoming `&amp;amp;`.
echo html`<p>{$inner}{$inner}</p>`, "\n";

// Which is the same answer `+` gives, because the splice is that operator in
// interpolation syntax.
echo html`<p>` + $inner + $inner + html`</p>`, "\n";

// The escape's own answer is a carrier too, so a hole holding it splices
// rather than escaping what the launderer already escaped.
Core\Html\Markup $escaped = Core\Html::escape($name);
echo html`<td>{$escaped}</td>`, "\n";

// A string hole beside a carrier hole is the contrast: one is data and is
// escaped, the other is markup and is not.
echo html`<td>{$name}</td><td>{$escaped}</td>`, "\n";
--EXPECT--
<p><em>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</em></p>
<p><em>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</em><em>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</em></p>
<p><em>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</em><em>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</em></p>
<td>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</td>
<td>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</td><td>ada &amp; &lt;grace&gt;</td>
